=== WOTS+ Key Reuse Attack Demo ===

Setup: WOTS+ key with 6 chains, each of length 16

Message 1 chain positions: {3, 7, 2, 5, 8, 4}
Message 2 chain positions: {7, 3, 9, 1, 6, 10}

Generating signature 1 (DANGEROUS - first use of key)...
Generating signature 2 (DANGEROUS - key reuse!)...

=== ATTACK: Forging a Third Signature ===

Target message positions: {5, 5, 8, 3, 7, 7}

Chain-by-chain forgery analysis:
  Chain 0: need position 5, have positions 3 and 7
    -> Forge from sig1: chain forward 2 steps
  Chain 1: need position 5, have positions 7 and 3
    -> Forge from sig2: chain forward 2 steps
  Chain 2: need position 8, have positions 2 and 9
    -> Forge from sig1: chain forward 6 steps
  Chain 3: need position 3, have positions 5 and 1
    -> Forge from sig2: chain forward 2 steps
  Chain 4: need position 7, have positions 8 and 6
    -> Forge from sig2: chain forward 1 steps
  Chain 5: need position 7, have positions 4 and 10
    -> Forge from sig1: chain forward 3 steps

=== Attack Result ===
FORGERY SUCCESSFUL!
Attacker created valid signature for new message.

=== Security Analysis ===

Key insight from the attack:
  - Signature reveals chain value at position m_i
  - Attacker can hash FORWARD (increase position)
  - Attacker CANNOT hash backward (would need preimage)

With ONE signature:
  - For each chain, attacker knows position m_i
  - Can forge any message where m'_i >= m_i for all i

With TWO signatures on different messages:
  - For each chain, attacker knows min(m1_i, m2_i)
  - Forgery space grows significantly

With multiple signatures:
  - Eventually know position 0 for each chain
  - Complete key recovery: can forge ANY message!

Conclusion: WOTS+ is ONE-TIME ONLY!
This is why SLH-DSA uses a hypertree of WOTS+ keys,
each used exactly once to sign the layer below.
